{"id":"5116caac-ea23-404f-947b-968f897d0655","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022912,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    id_to_node = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\\n    has_required = {}\\n    def dfs(node_id):\\n        node = id_to_node[node_id]\\n        req = node['symbol'] in required_symbols\\n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\\n            req = req or dfs(child_id)\\n        has_required[node_id] = req\\n        return req\\n    dfs('root')\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if has_required[nid]:\\n            continue\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        to_remove = []\\n        while stack:\\n            curr = stack.pop()\\n            if curr not in removed_set:\\n                to_remove.append(curr)\\n                for c in children.get(curr, []):\\n                    if c not in removed_set:\\n                        stack.append(c)\\n        for rid in to_remove:\\n            removed_set.add(rid)\\n            removed_order.append(rid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The case payload contains \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, returning removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nGiven description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have a grammar or interestingness oracle explicitly. However, we have \"required_symbols\" which must be preserved. Also \"removable\" flag indicates if node can be removed. \"grammar_valid\" likely means that after removal, the tree must still be a valid parse tree (i.e., parent-child relationships, but also symbol constraints: we need to ensure that required symbols are still present in the tree after removal). \"interestingness_preserved\" might be related to utility? Not sure. But the description says: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So the core condition: a node can be removed if it is removable, its removal doesn't cause loss of any required symbol, and presumably grammar valid? But grammar valid might also mean that removing a node should not orphan children? Actually in parse trees, removing a node typically means we remove its subtree? Or we remove the node and replace it with something? The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". There's also mention of \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\".\n\nGiven the limited info, we need to infer the logic from the test fixtures. Look at payloads: They have root with depth 0, removable false, utility 1.0. Children at depth 1: n1 (root,s1,removable false), n2 (root,s2,removable true), n3 (root,s0,removable false). Then at depth 2 children of those: n4 (n2,s1,removable true), n5 (n3,s2,removable false), n6 (n1,s0,removable true). Required symbols: root and s1.\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. Without specific expected output, we must deduce the algorithm.\n\nThe instructions say: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first). For each node, if removable, tentatively remove it. Check if all required symbols remain represented. If yes, mark it as removed? Also need to consider \"grammar_valid\" maybe means that after removal, the tree structure is still valid? If we remove a node, we might need to promote its children? Or simply remove the node and its subtree? Typically in parse tree reduction (like Perses), you can delete a node and its entire subtree if the resulting string (yield) still satisfies the grammar and interestingness? But here we don't have string yield.\n\nMaybe the problem is simpler: We have a tree of nodes. We want to remove as many removable nodes as possible while ensuring that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol remaining. Also, we cannot remove nodes that have retained children? Actually instruction: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node with retained children cannot be removed. That means when removing a node, all its descendants are also removed? Or if we remove a node, its children become roots? But then the tree is no longer a tree. Typically in parse tree reduction, if you delete a node, you delete its entire subtree. So children are also removed. That would mean if a node has children that are required or have required symbols, you cannot remove the node because those children would be lost. So the condition \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained (i.e., not removed), then you cannot remove the parent. So we need to process bottom-up.\n\nThus algorithm: Process nodes in decreasing depth. For each node (except root and those with required symbol), if it's removable, check if removing it and its subtree would cause loss of any required symbol. For required symbols, we need to check if the required symbol is still present somewhere else in the tree (outside this subtree). Also, if the node itself has a required symbol, we cannot remove it. But note: required symbols list includes \"root\" and \"s1\". So nodes with symbol \"root\" or \"s1\" are not removable. Also, after removing a node, its children are also removed, so any required symbol in that subtree is lost. So we can only remove a node if its entire subtree does not contain any required symbol (and the node itself is not required symbol). Then we can remove it.\n\nAdditionally, the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we check that after removal, every required symbol is still present in the whole tree (including root which is always present). So if a required symbol exists only in the subtree of the candidate node, removal is not allowed.\n\nAlso \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So if multiple nodes at same depth, order by node ID lexicographically? Probably ascend or descend? Since we process deeper first, at same depth we need a deterministic order. \"stable ties\" suggests using node ID as tiebreaker. We'll sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (or use order of appearance? but ID is string, we can compare).\n\nNow we need to compute removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. Remove nodes that meet conditions, retain all others.\n\nLet's test on the first fixture: payload with nodes list. Required: root, s1. Nodes:\nroot (id root, symbol root, removable false)\nn1 (symbol s1, removable false) - required symbol s1, so cannot remove.\nn2 (symbol s2, removable true) - not required, but has children: n4 (s1, removable true). n4 is required symbol s1, so cannot remove n2 because its subtree contains a required symbol (n4 has symbol s1). So n2 cannot be removed.\nn3 (symbol s0, removable false) - not removable.\nn4 (parent n2, symbol s1, removable true) - but symbol s1 is required, so cannot remove.\nn5 (parent n3, symbol s2, removable false) - not removable.\nn6 (parent n1, symbol s0, removable true) - not required. Its subtree: only itself. Remove it? Check if required symbols remain: root and s1 still present (root in root, s1 in n1). So n6 can be removed. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]? Then retained_node_ids: all others. But we must also consider that n6 is deep (depth 2). So process n6 first, remove it. Then process n4 (depth 2, removable true, but symbol s1, can't). Then process n5 (not removable). Then depth 1: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable true but subtree contains required s1, can't), n3 (not removable). So only n6 removed.\n\nWhat about n2's child n4? It cannot be removed because it is required. So result seems plausible.\n\nBut what about n2 itself? Could we remove n2 if we also remove n4? But n4 is required, cannot. So no.\n\nWhat about n6's removal? Its parent n1 is not removed. So retained children? n1 retains n6? But n6 is removed, so n1's child is removed. That's fine.\n\nNow check second fixture: same structure, different utilities but same required symbols. Should yield same removal pattern? Possibly n6 only.\n\nThird fixture: same.\n\nBut we need to be sure about the \"never remove a node with retained children\". This is implicitly enforced because if a node has retained children, those children are still there, so removing the node would leave children orphaned? Actually if we remove a node, its children are also removed. So if a child is retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent because the child is still retained and will be removed. So that condition is consistent.\n\nThus algorithm: Build a tree structure from nodes. Identify required set of symbols. Then iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, and within same depth by node ID ascending. For each node, if it is removable and its symbol is not in required_symbols, and if the subtree rooted at that node does not contain any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, then we can remove it. Removal means we exclude it from retained set, and also remove its entire subtree (so we should skip processing its descendants if we already removed the parent? But we process by descending depth, so children (deeper) will be processed before parents. However, if we remove a child, that doesn't affect parent's removal if parent's subtree still contains required symbols elsewhere. But if we remove a parent later, it will remove all its descendants (including children we might have already considered for removal). But we should not double-count removals. Better approach: Mark nodes as removed initially. Then process nodes bottom-up. For each node, we check if it can be removed given that its children may be removed or not. Actually if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree. So if some descendants are already marked removed (because they were removed individually), that's fine because they are already gone. But if we later remove the parent, we are removing them anyway. But we need to ensure that the removal of parent does not cause loss of required symbols that were still present in the subtree (perhaps from nodes we hadn't removed because they were required). So the condition is: after removing this node and its entire subtree, are all required symbols still present elsewhere in the tree? That depends on which other nodes (outside this subtree) have those required symbols.\n\nSo we need to know the distribution of required symbols among nodes. We can compute for each node: does its subtree contain any required symbol? If yes, cannot remove. Also the node itself if required symbol cannot be removed.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case.nodes and case.required_symbols.\n2. Build a dict from node ID to node object. Also build children lists per parent.\n3. Compute a set of required symbols.\n4. For each node, compute if its subtree (including itself) contains any required symbol. We can do a post-order traversal from root, or simply compute by checking recursively (since tree is small). We'll compute a set of required symbols present in subtree for each node. But we only need a boolean: whether subtree has required symbol. We'll compute a bool \"has_required\".\n5. Process nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node ID (str). For each node n:\n   - If n is root or n.symbol in required_symbols: skip.\n   - If not n.removable: skip.\n   - If n.has_required: skip (because subtree contains required symbol).\n   - Otherwise, we can remove it. Mark it as removed. Also, when we remove a node, all its descendants are effectively removed (even if they were not processed yet? But since we go descending depth, descendants are deeper and have been processed already. If a descendant was not removed (because it had required symbol), then removing the parent would remove it, which violates required symbol. But we already checked n.has_required, so no descendant has required. So all descendants either are not required and either removed or not? But we should ensure that if we remove parent, we also consider that descendants that were not removed (because they weren't removable or for other reasons) become removed. That's fine as they are not required. So we can just add the parent and all its descendants to removed set. However, we must not double-count. To simplify, we can mark all nodes in the subtree as removed (or ensure their IDs are in removed set). We'll accumulate set of removed_ids.\n6. After processing all nodes, define retained_node_ids as all node IDs not in removed_ids.\n7. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": sorted list of removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of retained_ids}. The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removed_node_ids should be in removal order? \"actual removal order\" suggests the order in which nodes were removed. So we need to keep track of the order of removal, not sorted. But the output schema says items type string, no order specified. But requirement: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" Possibly the removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (chronological), and retained_node_ids should be sorted. Let's check the problem: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" In output schema, removed_node_ids is an array of strings. It doesn't specify sorted. So likely we should output them in the order they were removed. But confirm: The schema only requires that it's an array of strings, no order constraint. However, the instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we should sort retained_node_ids and keep removal order for removed_node_ids.\n\nThus we need to track the removal order list.\n\nEdge cases: Root is never removed. Nodes that are not removable cannot be removed even if no required symbols in subtree. Also, if a node is removable and has no required subtree, we remove it. After removal, its parent's subtree may now have fewer required symbols? But we already computed has_required before any removal. However, if we remove a node that had a required symbol? No, we only remove if no required in subtree. When we remove a node, we might eliminate nodes that were themselves not required but were ancestors of required? No, because we check that the entire subtree has no required. So no required symbols are lost. So the parent's has_required is still true if parent itself has required or other children have required, but it doesn't matter because we check parent later. If parent later is considered, we check its has_required (which was computed before any removal). That includes the subtree of the removed node, which had no required. So it's still accurate because removing a node with no required symbol doesn't affect whether the rest of the parent's subtree has a required symbol. So pre-computation is fine.\n\nImplement solve(case):\n- Get nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build dict id_to_node.\n- Build children dict.\n- Compute has_required for each node using DFS from root. (Use recursion or stack)\n- Create list of all nodes sorted by depth descending, then node ID ascending.\n- removed_set = set()\n- removed_order = []\n- For each node in sorted_order:\n   if node id in removed_set: continue (if already removed as descendant of a previous parent removal)\n   if node.parent is None (root): continue\n   if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue (even if removable? Actually root is also required symbol, but root is already skipped. But what if there is another node with required symbol? Then we cannot remove it.)\n   if not node.removable: continue\n   if node_id in has_required_map and has_required_map[node_id]: continue\n   # can remove\n   # Mark all nodes in subtree as removed\n   # Use stack to collect all descendants\n   to_remove = [node_id]\n   stack = [node_id]\n   while stack:\n       curr = stack.pop()\n       for child in children.get(curr, []):\n           if child not in removed_set:\n               to_remove.append(child)\n               stack.append(child)\n   for rid in to_remove:\n       if rid not in removed_set:\n           removed_set.add(rid)\n           removed_order.append(rid)\n- retained_order = sorted([n.id for n in nodes if n.id not in removed_set])\n- return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_order}\n\nNow we need to ensure we treat the \"node\" objects as they are in the payload. They are dictionaries with keys: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. So we can access as dict.\n\nWrite code accordingly.\n\nTest with first fixture: We expect removed_order = [\"n6\"] only. But note: n6 is at depth 2, its parent n1. After removing n6, its parent n1's subtree no longer has n6, but that's fine. Later when processing n1 (depth 1), n1 is not removable, so no issue. For n2: its subtree has n4 which has symbol s1 (required), so has_required true. So n2 not removed. So result: removed [\"n6\"], retained sorted [root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6? Wait n6 is removed, so retained: all others except n6. Sorted: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root? root string \"root\" vs \"n1\" - \"root\" > \"n1\"? Lexicographically \"n1\" < \"root\" because 'n' is less than 'r'? Actually 'n' (110) vs 'r' (114), so \"n1\" < \"root\". So sorted order: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. But root is \"root\". So that's fine.\n\nBut wait: The fixture shows nodes list order: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6. Sorting retained IDs: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we should verify that we didn't incorrectly remove any other nodes. What about n5? Not removable. n4: required, not removed. Good.\n\nSecond fixture same structure, same result.\n\nThus algorithm seems consistent. However, is there any other condition like \"utility\" or \"interestingness oracle\"? The instructions say \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we are just implementing a reduction algorithm that ensures required symbols stay. The utility might be for something else but not used in this simple case? Might be for future tasks. So we ignore utility.\n\nAlso \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" might imply more complex operations like replacing a node with one of its children, but not here because we only have removable flag and no alternative candidates. So simple deletion is enough.\n\nThus final implementation as described.\n\nNow we need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source string. Must escape properly.\n\nLet's write the Python code. Use standard library only. No imports needed.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that takes case as dict? The interface says solve(case) is called with the payload object. So case is a dict with keys nodes (list of dicts) and required_symbols (list) and _case_nonce. We'll ignore nonce.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    id_to_node = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n['parent']\n        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\n    # compute has_required for each node\n    has_required = {}\n    # post-order traversal from root\n    def dfs(node_id):\n        node = id_to_node[node_id]\n        req = node['symbol'] in required_symbols\n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\n            req = req or dfs(child_id)\n        has_required[node_id] = req\n        return req\n    dfs('root')  # root exists\n    # sorted nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        if n['parent'] is None:  # root\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if has_required[nid]:\n            continue\n        # remove this node and all its descendants\n        stack = [nid]\n        to_remove = []\n        while stack:\n            curr = stack.pop()\n            if curr not in removed_set:\n                to_remove.append(curr)\n                for c in children.get(curr, []):\n                    if c not in removed_set:\n                        stack.append(c)\n        for rid in to_remove:\n            removed_set.add(rid)\n            removed_order.append(rid)\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nWe assume root exists and is named 'root'. The payloads always have root.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with escaped string. We'll craft the string.\n\nBut we must ensure the function returns a JSON-serializable object. That's fine.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nMake sure to include the exact JSON envelope.\n\nI'll write the output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2088,"completion_tokens":4959,"total_tokens":7047,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4515},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":40},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}